1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,750
Planck's trick was simple, and outrageous.

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:08,762
If energy comes in lumps, then a hot object can only emit light in certain sizes of lump.

3
00:00:08,762 --> 00:00:12,225
Small lumps when it is cool, bigger lumps as it heats up.

4
00:00:12,225 --> 00:00:13,675
The curve comes out right.

5
00:00:13,675 --> 00:00:14,762
Perfectly right.

6
00:00:14,762 --> 00:00:16,100
Embarrassingly right.

7
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:18,850
But a lump is a strange thing for nature to make.

8
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:20,812
A wave can be any size at all.

9
00:00:20,812 --> 00:00:23,937
A ripple in a pond can be a millimetre or a mile.

10
00:00:23,937 --> 00:00:26,325
A lump has to be exactly one lump.

11
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:30,850
In nineteen oh five, a young patent clerk in Bern took the idea seriously.

12
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:34,575
Albert Einstein argued that light itself is made of lumps.

13
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:35,587
Photons.

14
00:00:35,587 --> 00:00:45,487
And that this explains the photoelectric effect: shine light on a metal, and electrons come off, but only if the light is the right colour, no matter how bright it is.

15
00:00:45,487 --> 00:00:47,362
Brighter red light does nothing.

16
00:00:47,362 --> 00:00:49,487
Dim blue light works immediately.

17
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:52,012
Einstein called these packets light quanta.

18
00:00:52,012 --> 00:00:55,025
He did not call them photons; that word came later.

19
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:57,075
What matters is that he meant it literally.

20
00:00:57,075 --> 00:00:58,050
Not a trick.

21
00:00:58,050 --> 00:00:59,150
Not bookkeeping.

22
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:02,862
Light, he said, is a thing that arrives in pieces.

23
00:01:02,862 --> 00:01:05,300
He won the Nobel Prize for that, by the way.

24
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:06,637
Not for relativity.

25
00:01:06,637 --> 00:01:07,687
For the lumps.

26
00:01:07,687 --> 00:01:13,387
Then in nineteen thirteen, a young Dane named Niels Bohr pointed the same logic at the atom.

27
00:01:13,387 --> 00:01:18,362
Electrons, he said, cannot orbit a nucleus at any distance they like.

28
00:01:18,362 --> 00:01:20,500
They are only allowed certain orbits.

29
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,762
Certain energies.

30
00:01:21,762 --> 00:01:27,312
Jump between them, and the atom emits or absorbs a photon of exactly the right colour.

31
00:01:27,312 --> 00:01:28,187
It worked.

32
00:01:28,187 --> 00:01:36,525
Hydrogen's spectrum, those thin bright lines that had been measured in laboratories and puzzled over for decades, fell out of the arithmetic.

33
00:01:36,525 --> 00:01:38,950
The atom had a staircase, not a ramp.

34
00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:42,262
And then the staircase collapsed into something much stranger.

35
00:01:42,262 --> 00:01:47,162
In nineteen twenty-four, Louis de Broglie asked the question nobody had thought to ask.

36
00:01:47,162 --> 00:01:51,625
If waves can behave like particles, why shouldn't particles behave like waves?

37
00:01:51,625 --> 00:01:54,575
An electron, he suggested, is a wave.

38
00:01:54,575 --> 00:01:57,312
A tiny, restless, standing wave.

39
00:01:57,312 --> 00:02:04,462
Three years later, in nineteen twenty-seven, physicists took a photograph that made the idea impossible to ignore.

40
00:02:04,462 --> 00:02:10,450
In Aberdeen, a young man named George Paget Thomson fired electrons at a thin metal foil.

41
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:11,887
They came out as rings.

42
00:02:11,887 --> 00:02:13,262
Diffraction rings.

43
00:02:13,262 --> 00:02:14,887
The kind you get from waves.

44
00:02:14,887 --> 00:02:16,450
The kind you get from light.

45
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:17,950
Electrons were waves.

46
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:19,237
And they were particles.

47
00:02:19,237 --> 00:02:20,362
At the same time.

48
00:02:20,362 --> 00:02:21,900
Depending on how you looked.

49
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:28,000
That photograph is one of the most important images in the history of science, and almost nobody has seen it.

50
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,525
By then, the theory had already been rewritten twice.

51
00:02:31,525 --> 00:02:41,637
In nineteen twenty-five, Werner Heisenberg, stranded on a windswept island in the North Sea with hay fever, invented a mathematics in which quantities do not commute.

52
00:02:41,637 --> 00:02:45,150
Where A times B is not the same as B times A.

53
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:46,675
He did not know what it meant.

54
00:02:46,675 --> 00:02:48,125
Neither did anyone else.

55
00:02:48,125 --> 00:02:55,537
A year later, Erwin Schrodinger wrote down an equation that described the electron as a wave, spreading through space.

56
00:02:55,537 --> 00:02:59,937
It was beautiful, and it was wrong in the way that all useful things are wrong.

57
00:02:59,937 --> 00:03:01,262
It described the wave.

58
00:03:01,262 --> 00:03:03,412
It did not describe what the wave was.

59
00:03:03,412 --> 00:03:06,225
Max Born supplied the answer that nobody wanted.

60
00:03:06,225 --> 00:03:08,100
The wave is not the electron.

61
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:09,725
The wave is a probability.

62
00:03:09,725 --> 00:03:13,837
Square it, and you get the odds of finding the electron here rather than there.

63
00:03:13,837 --> 00:03:16,462
Nature, at bottom, is a casino.

64
00:03:16,462 --> 00:03:17,837
Einstein hated that.

65
00:03:17,837 --> 00:03:22,900
He wrote to Born in nineteen twenty-six: "Quantum mechanics is certainly imposing.

66
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:26,200
But an inner voice tells me that it is not yet the real thing.

67
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:31,500
The theory says a lot, but does not really bring us any closer to the secret of the old one.

68
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:35,875
I, at any rate, am convinced that He is not playing at dice."

69
00:03:35,875 --> 00:03:36,862
He was wrong.

70
00:03:36,862 --> 00:03:37,887
Or he was right.

71
00:03:37,887 --> 00:03:39,012
We still don't know.

72
00:03:39,012 --> 00:03:42,662
In nineteen twenty-seven, the two sides met in Brussels.

73
00:03:42,662 --> 00:03:44,562
The fifth Solvay Conference.

74
00:03:44,562 --> 00:03:49,987
Twenty-nine physicists in one room, seventeen of whom would win Nobel Prizes.

75
00:03:49,987 --> 00:04:01,212
The photograph from that week is the most famous group portrait in science: Einstein in the front row, Bohr beside him, Marie Curie between them, Heisenberg and Pauli standing behind.

76
00:04:01,322 --> 00:04:04,597
They argued about whether the universe is deterministic.

77
00:04:04,597 --> 00:04:09,384
Einstein kept proposing thought experiments designed to break quantum mechanics.

78
00:04:09,384 --> 00:04:13,584
Bohr kept answering them, sometimes overnight, sometimes at breakfast.

79
00:04:13,584 --> 00:04:20,159
And in nineteen thirty-five, Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen published the sharpest version of the objection.

80
00:04:20,159 --> 00:04:24,472
Take two particles that interact, then separate them by a light-year.

81
00:04:24,472 --> 00:04:27,884
Measure one, and you instantly know something about the other.

82
00:04:27,884 --> 00:04:29,447
Not because you disturbed it.

83
00:04:29,447 --> 00:04:32,709
Because the theory says the two were never really separate.

84
00:04:32,709 --> 00:04:35,584
Einstein called it spooky action at a distance.

85
00:04:35,584 --> 00:04:37,097
He meant it as an insult.

86
00:04:37,097 --> 00:04:38,297
It was a measurement.

